    "q-350445": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350445",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x).$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{64}-⋯$",
      "worked_solution": "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4(1-\\frac{3x}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\frac{3x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin series for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-\\frac{3x}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\frac{3x}{4}) & =(-\\frac{3x}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{3x}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{(-\\frac{3x}{4})}^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{27{x}^{3}}{64})-⋯ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{32}-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{64}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\frac{3x}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+(-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{32}-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{64}-⋯) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{32}-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{64}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
