    "q-347905": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347905",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+x}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{4}+⋯$",
      "worked_solution": "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}-⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{5}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+x} & =1+(-x)+(-x)^{2}+(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+x},$ we multiply the expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ by the expansion of $\\frac{1}{1+x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+x}=(x-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{5}-⋯)(1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single $x$ term, given by\n\n$$\nx\\cdot 1=x.\n$$\n\n- There is a single ${x}^{2}$ term, given by\n\n$$\nx\\cdot (-x)=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- There are two ${x}^{3}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})\\cdot 1 & ={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first three non-zero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+x}=x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$",
