      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of ${cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we multiply the expansion by itself, as follows:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)(1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n- There is a single constant term, given by\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- There are two ${x}^{2}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- There are three ${x}^{4}$ terms, and their sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4})\\cdot 1+(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first three nonzero terms of the expansion are\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$",
